 This formulation also explains why a tempting early hypothesis failed. Integer anchor overlap has the right overall trend but is not a monotone dose: one weak shared anchor can matter less than another, and an exact four-anchor ladder reverses at zero-to-one overlap. We therefore replace count by estimator influence. For analytical interpolation this is derived from coefficients. For frozen HAT and SwinIR networks it is approximated by the cosine between output responses to identical finite perturbations of the same physical anchor. The quantity is explicitly local to a target, context, input, and plane.
 
 Our contributions are threefold. First, we establish and decompose \emph{consensus blindness} with same-target, measurement-controlled interventions across three MRI cohorts and a prospectively frozen five-architecture replication on two independent brain cohorts. Second, we show that local estimator influence refines raw overlap for analytical estimators and stable HAT/SwinIR/SAINT probes, while retaining the SAINT fixed-count uncertainty, NingXia nonreplication, physical-plane dependence, and I3Net/CycleINR stability failures as scope boundaries. Third, we test a lightweight acquired-anchor consequence with same-model $\times4\!\rightarrow\!\times2$ comparisons across a recovered model bank, without claiming score uniqueness or acquisition causality. The main result is a falsifiable mechanism for when disagreement is informative, not another uncertainty leaderboard.
 
