 \section{Conclusion}
-Deterministic MRI interpolators can agree while jointly wrong because shared error cancels from prediction differences. Across three cohorts and five neural architectures, same-target interventions show that dependence-preserving contexts hide more error than disjoint contexts. The architecture tests support a local physical-dependence--error-geometry mechanism, but neither universal ranking improvement nor universally stable finite-response influence. Raw same-model benefit associations are positive for most models, but current-error adjustment and acquired-range normalization expose important limits. Operational value still requires prospective validation for the acquisition and endpoint.
+Shared error can make deterministic MRI interpolators agree while jointly wrong. Across three cohorts and five neural architectures, same-target interventions support a local measurement-dependence mechanism, with cohort-dependent ranking and architecture-dependent influence stability. Retrospective benefit associations weaken after current-error adjustment or scale changes. Prospective acquisition and endpoint validation remain necessary.
 
